{"title": "Best Buy brings back 4K Blu-ray discs with an expanding collection, in-store availability [U]", "link": "https://9to5google.com/2026/11/20/best-buy-brings-back-4k-blu-ray-discs-with-a-slowly-expanding-collection/", "summary": "<div class=\"feat-image\"><img src=\"https://9to5google.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/2020/03/Best-Buy-Store-Front.jpg?quality=82&strip=all&w=1600\" /></div><p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">It’s been nearly three years since Best Buy decided to ditch selling physical copies of movies, stripping its inventory from both its brick-and-mortar stores and its online catalog. But with the growing rise of 4K Blu-ray collections as a vinyl-esque collector’s item, the electronics giant has slowly begun dipping its toes back into the physical media game.</p>\n\n\n\n <a class=\"more-link\" href=\"https://9to5google.com/2026/11/20/best-buy-brings-back-4k-blu-ray-discs-with-a-slowly-expanding-collection/#more-738444\">more…</a>", "published": "2026-11-20T11:39:35+00:00", "source": "9to5Google", "category": "subscription", "matched_entities": {}, "logged_at": "2026-11-22T17:44:42.485929+00:00", "ontology": {"repo": "SubscriptionRadar", "category": "subscription", "ontology_version": "0.1.0", "evidence_policy_ids": ["traceable_url_required", "cross_reference_required", "non_authoritative_context_only"]}}
{"title": "Google Health update fixes my biggest Fitbit Air complaint with ‘in-progress’ workout card", "link": "https://9to5google.com/2026/11/20/google-health-fitbit-air-workouts-in-progress-card/", "summary": "<div class=\"feat-image\"><img src=\"https://9to5google.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/2026/10/Fitbit-Air-Steph-Curry-.jpg?quality=82&strip=all&w=1600\" /></div><p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Google’s Fitbit Air is one of my favorite products of 2026 and my preferred workout companion, but not being able to see a workout while you’re in the middle of it has been a huge complaint of mine, and the latest Google Health update is fixing that.</p>\n\n\n\n <a class=\"more-link\" href=\"https://9to5google.com/2026/11/20/google-health-fitbit-air-workouts-in-progress-card/#more-739324\">more…</a>", "published": "2026-11-20T10:40:00+00:00", "source": "9to5Google", "category": "subscription", "matched_entities": {}, "logged_at": "2026-11-22T17:44:42.485929+00:00", "ontology": {"repo": "SubscriptionRadar", "category": "subscription", "ontology_version": "0.1.0", "evidence_policy_ids": ["traceable_url_required", "cross_reference_required", "non_authoritative_context_only"]}}
{"title": "Is the Galaxy S26 Ultra still a good buy at the end of 2026?", "link": "https://9to5google.com/2026/11/20/is-the-galaxy-s26-ultra-still-a-good-buy-at-the-end-of-2026/", "summary": "<div class=\"feat-image\"><img src=\"https://9to5google.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/2026/03/galaxy-s26-ultra-review-11.jpg?quality=82&strip=all&w=1600\" /></div><p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Yes, the <a href=\"https://bestbuy.7tiv.net/c/126147/614286/10014?subId1=danielbader&subId2=E4C300&sharedid=danielbader&u=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.bestbuy.com%2Fproduct%2Fsamsung-galaxy-s26-ultra-256gb-unlocked-black%2FJJGRF36YGZ&partnerpropertyid=8424468\">Samsung Galaxy S26 Ultra</a> remains a good buy in late 2026 if you want a large-screen Android phone with versatile cameras, an integrated S Pen, and long-term software support. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Galaxy S26 Ultra has aged very well, benefiting from Samsung’s mature hardware and software, along with a host of other quality-of-life improvements gained from its recent update to One UI 9 running Android 17.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We’re going to take a deeper dive at what the S26 Ultra is, and how it compares to its cheaper siblings, in a quest to figure out whether it’s still worth your time and money this holiday season.</p>\n\n\n\n <a class=\"more-link\" href=\"https://9to5google.com/2026/11/20/is-the-galaxy-s26-ultra-still-a-good-buy-at-the-end-of-2026/#more-736288\">more…</a>", "published": "2026-11-20T10:12:42+00:00", "source": "9to5Google", "category": "subscription", "matched_entities": {}, "logged_at": "2026-11-22T17:44:42.485929+00:00", "ontology": {"repo": "SubscriptionRadar", "category": "subscription", "ontology_version": "0.1.0", "evidence_policy_ids": ["traceable_url_required", "cross_reference_required", "non_authoritative_context_only"]}}
{"title": "Google Health 5.13 rolling out: Improved food search, more swimming controls", "link": "https://9to5google.com/2026/11/20/google-health-5-13-release-notes/", "summary": "<div class=\"feat-image\"><img src=\"https://9to5google.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/2026/05/google-health-logo-1.jpg?quality=82&strip=all&w=1600\" /></div><p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Version 5.13 of Google Health is <a href=\"https://support.google.com/googlehealth/thread/478477332\">rolling out</a> to <a href=\"https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.fitbit.FitbitMobile\">Android</a> and <a href=\"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/google-health-fitbit/id462638897\">iOS</a> with updates across five areas. It’s a modest release that reiterates recent launches.</p>\n\n\n\n <a class=\"more-link\" href=\"https://9to5google.com/2026/11/20/google-health-5-13-release-notes/#more-739327\">more…</a>", "published": "2026-11-20T10:00:55+00:00", "source": "9to5Google", "category": "subscription", "matched_entities": {}, "logged_at": "2026-11-22T17:44:42.485929+00:00", "ontology": {"repo": "SubscriptionRadar", "category": "subscription", "ontology_version": "0.1.0", "evidence_policy_ids": ["traceable_url_required", "cross_reference_required", "non_authoritative_context_only"]}}
{"title": "Google retiring Nest app and Google Assistant on speakers, displays in 2027", "link": "https://9to5google.com/2026/11/20/nest-app-google-assistant/", "summary": "<div class=\"feat-image\"><img src=\"https://9to5google.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/2023/11/nest-app-pixel-1.jpg?quality=82&strip=all&w=1600\" /></div><p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Following the <a href=\"https://9to5google.com/2026/09/28/google-assistant-gemini-android/\">Android deprecation</a> in September, Google Assistant is <a href=\"https://support.google.com/googlehome/blog/478236207\">going away</a> on speakers and smart displays next year. Additionally, Google Home is fully replacing the Nest app in January.</p>\n\n\n\n <a class=\"more-link\" href=\"https://9to5google.com/2026/11/20/nest-app-google-assistant/#more-739128\">more…</a>", "published": "2026-11-20T09:00:00+00:00", "source": "9to5Google", "category": "subscription", "matched_entities": {}, "logged_at": "2026-11-22T17:44:42.485929+00:00", "ontology": {"repo": "SubscriptionRadar", "category": "subscription", "ontology_version": "0.1.0", "evidence_policy_ids": ["traceable_url_required", "cross_reference_required", "non_authoritative_context_only"]}}
{"title": "Clicks Communicator hits a supply chain snag, and it isn’t because of RAM", "link": "https://9to5google.com/2026/11/20/clicks-communicator-minor-timeline-delay/", "summary": "<div class=\"feat-image\"><img src=\"https://9to5google.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/2026/01/Clicks-CES-2026-28-of-30.jpg?quality=82&strip=all&w=1600\" /></div><p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Clicks is still on track to release the phone with a physical keyboard within the next few months, but a Communicator launch before the end of this year isn’t looking very likely as shipping gets pushed back.</p>\n\n\n\n <a class=\"more-link\" href=\"https://9to5google.com/2026/11/20/clicks-communicator-minor-timeline-delay/#more-739296\">more…</a>", "published": "2026-11-20T08:51:23+00:00", "source": "9to5Google", "category": "subscription", "matched_entities": {}, "logged_at": "2026-11-22T17:44:42.485929+00:00", "ontology": {"repo": "SubscriptionRadar", "category": "subscription", "ontology_version": "0.1.0", "evidence_policy_ids": ["traceable_url_required", "cross_reference_required", "non_authoritative_context_only"]}}
